From: fnerd@smds.com (FutureNerd Steve Witham)
Date: Tue, 28 Jun 94 19:13:08 EDT
Subject: [#94-6-189] Scenarios: What happens when nanotechnology appears?

Robin sees nanotech as not-unlike any other tech in fueling
arms races.

Nanotech looks more risky because it hasn't happened yet--
but that doesn't invalidate fear of nano.  Nano may 
be just one more risky, unpredictable stoking of the overall
arms race, but just because we've survived so far doesn't 
mean the next adjustment won't be the last.

I think since before nano we've been decreasing the "time-to-
market" of weapons technologies.  We're
learning to ramp up for more widespread destruction faster.
I don't know how to measure that, or how to measure the
speed with which wars are decided (at a given rate of 
killing, the sooner the war ends, the less killing), but I 
guess that's the ratio to look at.  If we can give everybody 
a mass destruction weapon in one day, but it takes two days
for them to negotiate peace, it may be too late.

How coupled are weapons-production and war-resolution rates?
("Weapons" measured in terms of killing potential.)  Is
war-resolution tied to effectiveness or speed of the weapons
themselves?  My first guess is that war-resolution is
held back by the speed of people's brains, while technology
increases what can be done without brains.  

Things like computers and communications probably speed up
war-resolution.  But I guess brain speed holds back how far
and how fast this effect can go.  Or am I wrong?

Widespread nano factories that need only the latest software, 
and, later, self-reproducing nanotech, would seem to increase 
the tool speed/brain speed gap. 

At some point we'll have uploading and AI and tech will speed 
up brains as well, but for now it looks to me like weapons are 
evolving faster than soldiers and statesmen.  I'm not even
sure brain tech can ever accelerate at the same rate that
brain-free weapons tech can.

I forgot to measure "wisdom".  How does awareness of
how to deal with the facts of weapons and war advance?  Is
it tied to the other variables by some Darwinian rule?
Maybe he who adapts fast enough wins, and if nobody adapts, 
you get a bad war, slowed economies and slowed tech 
advancement?

Is "wisdom" being incorporated into the smarts of weapons?
Necessarily?

-fnerd
