From: sullivan@blaze.cs.jhu.edu (Gregory Sullivan)
Date: Wed, 29 Jun 94 14:11:50 EDT
Subject: [#94-6-197] Scenarios: What happens when nanotechnology appears? 

Steve Witham makes some excellent points that I agree with about changes in
uncertainty in the future. He raises several of the key issues including the
effect of AI.

Peter McCluskey, raises some good hard questions.  He argues using recent
history that uncertainty is decreasing.

> I disagree with the claim that there is or will be a trend towards
>increasing uncertainty in the development of military hardware.
>it is likely that change in this area will accelerate (although I see
>no signs that this is happening yet), the improvements in information
>gathering abilities have substantially reduced uncertainties over the
>past few decades, and all indications are that this trend will continue
>to offset the problems associated with faster hardware development. For
>instance, there was a big debate in the late 50's about a missile gap
>between the US and the USSR. That largely vanished about 1960 when spy
>planes and satellites became good enough to count missiles reliably.

It is hard to argue from complex recent history but since Peter
tried so will I ;-).

My conclusions from recent history are different.  During the Cuban missile
crisis there was an unprecedented amount of uncertainty. We were close to
nuclear armageddon.  The amount of damage the human species might have
inflicted upon itself was also unprecedented.  This uncertainty was due to
technological innovations, especially missiles, and nuclear bombs.  I do not
claim that uncertainty in military interactions will simplistically
monotonically increase over time.  I do think the overall modern trend is up.

What about our information gathering capabilities?  I agree with Peter that
they have gotten better (but see below).  Note, the missile gap was a
politically motivated distortion of actually intelligence information.  I am
not sure what the true estimates were. Despite this I will grant you that
our knowledge of land based Soviet misslies became more accurate.

It is hard to point to a single technological change and claim that
uncertainty has decreased because of it as you have done above.  Consider
some other technological changes:

The United States introduced MIRVed missiles on submarines.  Neither
satellite or plane reconnaissance can count these warheads.

Nuclear cruise missiles were introduced. They are also hard to count.

The yield and accuracy of nuclear missiles has increased because of
technological innovations, especially computers. Some analysts believe that
land based missiles are vulnerable to a first strike.  Some analysts wanted
to move to a computerized launch on warning system.

The time for a Russian(Former Soviet) missiles to hit US cities has
decreased.

During the Gulf war the US was unable to accurately determine the number or
location of Iraqs Scuds. Russia has many mobile nuclear missiles deployed.

Only fragmentary information is known (in the open literature at least)
about the Russian (Former Soviet) biological weapons program.  Some
unpleasant info is available.  There was an explosion in Russia of a site
thought to be a biowar facility.  Anthrax was released and many were
killed.  (In my earlier post and in this one I include biological warfare
agents under the rubric military hardware.)

The US was unable to tell beforehand if Iraqs Scuds would contain a
conventional, chemical, biological or nuclear warhead.

Has uncertainty increased or decreased?  Are we more stable or less stable
now?  I think we are more stable now because of the collapse of the Soviet
Union. That is a socioeconomic development not a technological one.

Nuclear, biological and chemical weapons are all now proliferating.
Technology continues to advance. I suspect even prenanotech uncertainty will
increase unless we can avoid a war environment.


